  "body": "The Probe op (#108, #178) posts only through `assets/posts/fanuc.cps` (G31). The Tormach PathPilot post (`assets/posts/tormach.cps`) writes no probing blocks, so a Probe op on a Tormach machine is refused by name at post time. PathPilot is LinuxCNC-based and probes with **G38.2 / G38.3 / G38.4 / G38.5**, not G31.\n\n**Proposed**\n- Give `tormach.cps` the probing hooks the Fanuc post has:\n  - the probing move as `G38.2` (or `G38.3` where the op wants a non-erroring seek, which pairs with #174's protected descent);\n  - the slow re-probe;\n  - the probing result that sets the work offset (`G10 L20 P<n>` from the measured position, the LinuxCNC way).\n- The specifics must come from Tormach's published PathPilot / operator documentation and the LinuxCNC G-code reference, never from other CAM output (clean-room). Check in particular:\n  - which probe input PathPilot expects;\n  - how a failed probe alarms;\n  - whether PathPilot's own probing cycles should be preferred over raw G38.x.\n- Cover face, edge, outside-corner and bore/boss modes, the same set the Fanuc post covers.\n\n**Done means**\n- A Probe op posts on the Tormach 770MX / 1100MX machines instead of being refused.\n- Goldens cover each probing mode.\n- The posted program runs clean in PathPilot Hub (the 770MX simulator). The contributor can run this check.\n\nRelated: #173 (probing hooks for Haas, Mazak, Siemens, Heidenhain), #174 (G38.3-style protected descent), #171 / PR #350 (probe from a pick). Part of #131 (Tormach support).\n",
